Senegal is preparing to host the Youth Olympic Games, which begin this weekend. The young athletes who hope to showcase their talents on the international stage have their sights set on the Games, and Kenya has made sure that its young boxers remain on their toes and are ready to take on opponents of a higher calibre. FRANCE 24's Bastien Renouil reports from the ground.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f5e0bd6-d381-11f1-9ace-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f8d19214.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f5e0bd6-d381-11f1-9ace-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f8d19214.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">27406a60-d37f-11f1-9cc5-5550795cf039</guid><pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:45:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'There's clearly a marketplace for Russian in Africa - but it's not about institution building'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-there-s-clearly-a-marketplace-for-russian-in-africa-but-it-s-not-about-institution-building</link><description>
Oliver Farry is pleased to welcome Alex Vines, Africa Program Director at the European Council on Foreign Relations. He takes us through Russia's African paradox involving diplomatic Influence, security dependence and the politics of multipolarity. Moscow is gaining diplomatic traction across the continent even as its security partnerships, information operations and recruitment practices raise troubling questions about sovereignty, accountability and the human costs of geopolitical competition. For Vines, the participation of 48 African countries and 22 heads of state and government in the latest Russia-Africa summit illustrates the continuing appeal,  of Moscow as a diplomatic and strategic partner.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b68a66e4-d37f-11f1-9402-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261029-081049-081833-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b68a66e4-d37f-11f1-9402-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261029-081049-081833-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bbf072a4-d37f-11f1-b1fa-a939b9b14960</guid><pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:43:25 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Somalia: Lower house of parliament passes bill on sexual violence</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-somalia-lower-house-of-parliament-passes-bill-on-sexual-violence</link><description>
The Somali Parliament has passed a new law against sexual violence. The country’s criminal code dated back to 1962 and had not been updated since. FRANCE 24's Bastien Renouil met with Sofia Rajab in Nairobi. She is the program manager at the NGO Legal Action Worldwide, an organisation that lobbied Somali authorities in support of this new law.
 

 

